    <!-- Section 1: What Happens in the Diagnostic -->
    <section class="section">
        <div class="container">
            <h2 class="section-title">What Happens in the 60 Minutes</h2>
            <p class="section-intro">The diagnostic is a written assessment that tests concepts from your child's current grade AND previous grades. We're not checking if they can get the right answer — we're watching how they approach the problem.</p>
            <p class="section-intro">Do they set it up correctly? Do they know which method to use? Where exactly does the thinking break down? Is the problem in algebra, or does it go deeper — to fractions, to integers, to basic number sense?</p>
            <p class="section-intro">After the written part, we solve every question together with your child. This is not a pass/fail test. It's a conversation. Your child sees exactly where they went wrong and why. They experience how we teach — patient, step-by-step, focused on understanding rather than answers.</p>
            <p class="section-intro">By the end of the session, three things are clear:</p>
            <ul class="feature-list">
                <li><strong>Where exactly the gaps are</strong> — not "weak in maths" but specifically which concepts are missing.</li>
                <li><strong>What needs to happen first</strong> — the sequence of topics that need rebuilding.</li>
                <li><strong>Whether we're the right fit</strong> — the diagnostic is as much for your child as it is for us.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 2: What Parents Learn -->
    <section class="section bg-light">
        <div class="container">
            <h2 class="section-title">What You'll Know After the Diagnostic</h2>
            <div class="offer-grid">
                <div class="offer-card">
                    <h3>Where the Gaps Actually Are</h3>
                    <p>Not just "they're weak in algebra." We'll tell you specifically — they understand basic equations but break down when variables appear on both sides because fractions were never solid.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="offer-card">
                    <h3>What Needs to Happen First</h3>
                    <p>Foundation repair has an order. We identify exactly which concepts need to be rebuilt and in what sequence — so nothing is wasted.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="offer-card">
                    <h3>Whether This Is the Right Fit</h3>
                    <p>The diagnostic is as much for your child as it is for us. They experience how we teach. You see how we think. If it doesn't feel right for either side, that's completely fine.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 3: What the First Month Looks Like -->
    <section class="section">
        <div class="container">
            <h2 class="section-title">What Happens After the Diagnostic</h2>
            <p class="section-intro">If we're the right fit, here's how the first month typically looks:</p>
            <div class="offer-grid" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
                <div class="offer-card">
                    <h3>Week 1</h3>
                    <p>The diagnostic. After the session, we discuss findings with you — specific gaps, recommended plan, batch timing, frequency.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="offer-card">
                    <h3>Week 2</h3>
                    <p>First classes begin. Not from the textbook, but from the foundation. We go back to exactly where things stopped making sense. This might feel slow. It isn't. This is the most important phase.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="offer-card">
                    <h3>Week 3</h3>
                    <p>Homework attempts start flowing in via WhatsApp photos. Your child comes back with specific questions — or stuck on something they couldn't crack. That's perfect. That's exactly what we want to see.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="offer-card">
                    <h3>Week 4</h3>
                    <p>You'll notice something shift. "I don't understand maths" becomes "I don't understand why we flip the inequality sign." That shift — from vague fear to precise doubt — is the first sign it's working.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section: Sample Diagnostic Report -->
    <section class="section bg-light">
        <div class="container">
            <h2 class="section-title">What the Diagnostic Report Looks Like</h2>
            <p class="section-intro">After the 60-minute diagnostic evaluation, parents receive a detailed assessment document that goes far beyond a standard report card. Instead of basic percentages, we break down the student's mathematical foundation into specific areas:</p>
            <div class="content-grid">
                <div class="content-item">
                    <h3>Arithmetic Fluency</h3>
                    <p>Speed and accuracy with basic operations, fractions, decimals, and integers</p>
                </div>
                <div class="content-item">
                    <h3>Algebraic Manipulation</h3>
                    <p>Factorisation, simplification, equation-solving habits</p>
                </div>
                <div class="content-item">
                    <h3>Spatial Reasoning</h3>
                    <p>Geometry intuition, diagram skills, coordinate understanding</p>
                </div>
                <div class="content-item">
                    <h3>Problem Solving</h3>
                    <p>Ability to translate word problems into mathematical setups</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p class="section-intro" style="margin-top: 2rem;">The report pinpoints the exact areas where students get stuck — for example, not just saying they "struggle with fractions," but showing they have trouble finding common denominators with variables. We conclude the report with a specific, week-by-week repair plan to reconstruct their base knowledge.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section: Common Gaps We Find -->
    <section class="section">
        <div class="container">
            <h2 class="section-title">Common Gaps We Find</h2>
            <p class="section-intro">Over years of teaching, incredibly consistent patterns emerge when it comes to weak basics. The rush to complete school syllabi often forces students forward before they are ready.</p>
            <div class="offer-grid" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
                <div class="offer-card">
                    <h3>Class 10 students</h3>
                    <p>Most <a href="/cbse-tuition-jubilee-hills">CBSE</a> and <a href="/icse-tuition-hyderabad">ICSE</a> Class 10 students who struggle with advanced topics have gaps in Class 7–8 algebra — things like fractional manipulation and basic factorisation.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="offer-card">
                    <h3>Class 9 students</h3>
                    <p>Students transitioning to rigorous physics often show an inability to transpose formulas because they memorised linear arithmetic instead of understanding mathematical logic.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="offer-card">
                    <h3>International board students</h3>
                    <p><a href="/igcse-tuition-jubilee-hills">IGCSE</a> and <a href="/ib-myp-tuition-hyderabad">IB MYP</a> students often struggle not with the concepts themselves, but with communicating their reasoning clearly — a skill that directly affects their grades.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p class="section-intro" style="margin-top: 2rem;">Finding and fixing these exact gaps during the ₹750 assessment prevents a total collapse of confidence later on. Read more about <a href="/how-we-teach">how we teach</a> once we've identified the gaps.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

